window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][22]={"university_physics_en:M22-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M22-L1 – Brownian Motion, Diffusion and Time Scales<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Bachelor: Biophysics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M5: thermodynamics; M13: statistical\nphysics; M17: stochastic simulations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model molecular diffusion as a\nrandom walk, derive mean-square displacement and estimate diffusion\ntimes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>A fluorescently labelled protein moves irregularly even in a liquid\nwith no net flow. Many collisions with surrounding molecules produce its\nfluctuating trajectory. One observed path therefore does not represent a\nsingle deterministic velocity; the distribution of many trajectories is\nmore informative. Thermal motion can smooth a concentration gradient\nwithout requiring bulk fluid motion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>Consider independent, zero-mean displacements with finite variance\nalong one dimension. Variances add: after\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsteps,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle(\\Delta x)^2\\rangle=n\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=n\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=\\sigma^2/(2\\Delta t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis yields<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>D<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>c<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle[x(t)-x(0)]^2\\rangle=2Dt,\\qquad \\partial_t c=D\\nabla^2 c.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For independent motion in three dimensions, the corresponding\nmean-square displacement is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mi>D<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6Dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe continuum limit gives the diffusion equation. Under the Stokes\nassumptions for a rigid sphere in a dilute Newtonian fluid with no slip,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>η<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=k_{\\mathrm B}T/(6\\pi\\eta r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napproximately; this relation is not universal for intracellular\nproteins. The typical one-dimensional time to diffuse over a length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nscales as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\sim L^2/(2D)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>For a freely diffusing ideal probe with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.50\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m^2/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nobserved for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=4\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe root-mean-square displacement is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{\\langle\\Delta x^2\\rangle}=\\sqrt{2\\cdot0.50\\cdot4}\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}=2.0\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram depicts the theoretical MSD and the spreading of an\nidealised concentration profile, not recorded cell trajectories.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_032_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Plot of linear mean-square displacement and a widening concentration profile caused by diffusion.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Plot of linear mean-square displacement\nand a widening concentration profile caused by diffusion.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Synthetic diffusion models, not measurements from living\ncells.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the one-dimensional RMS displacement for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.8\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m^2/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=10\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Estimate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=10\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=1\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m^2/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle\\Delta x\\rangle=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhile\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle\\Delta x^2\\rangle&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>D<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>16<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2Dt}=\\sqrt{16}\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}=4\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\sim L^2/(2D)=100/2\\,\\mathrm s=50\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Positive and negative steps cancel in the mean but contribute\nnon-negative squares to the variance.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>Stokes-Einstein assumes a continuum solvent, negligible inertia,\nspherical particles and weak interactions. Crowding, binding, active\ntransport and anomalous diffusion can change cellular trajectories. The\npictured concentration profile is model-generated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>For normal diffusion, mean-square displacement grows linearly with\ntime. The squared length scale sets the order of magnitude of diffusion\ntimes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Molecular Binding, Chemical\nPotential and Cooperativity<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-592j-statistical-physics-in-biology-spring-2011/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Statistical Physics in Biology – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: chapter outline lines 34–66; Subject relevance to lesson: M22-L1 – Brownian Motion, Diffusion and Time Scales · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M22-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M22-L2 – Molecular Binding, Chemical Potential and Cooperativity<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Bachelor: Biophysics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M5: free energy; M13: partition\nfunction; M22-L1: thermal fluctuations. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive a binding isotherm,\nevaluate a dissociation constant and interpret cooperativity\ncautiously.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>A ligand may attach to a protein and subsequently detach. When the\nfree ligand concentration is held fixed, reversible binding reaches a\ndynamic equilibrium in which binding and unbinding events balance\nstatistically. A high occupancy is not evidence that a given ligand\nremains bound indefinitely.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>For independent identical sites in the simple two-state reaction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo>⇌<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P+L\\rightleftharpoons PL<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith dilute ideal species,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_d=[P][L]/[PL]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=[L]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the free, rather than the total, ligand concentration. The occupied\nfraction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=[PL]/([P]+[PL])<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=\\frac{c}{K_d+c},\\qquad K_d=\\frac{k_{\\mathrm{off}}}{k_{\\mathrm{on}}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For the simplest mass-action kinetic scheme,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_d=k_{\\mathrm{off}}/k_{\\mathrm{on}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncarries concentration units. The expression\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msup><mi>G<\/mi><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mi>K<\/mi><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta G^\\circ=-RT\\ln K^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrequires a dimensionless standard-state equilibrium constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>K<\/mi><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA steeper empirical curve is sometimes described by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>c<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/msub><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msubsup><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/msub><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>c<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/msub><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=c^{n_H}/(K_{1/2}^{n_H}+c^{n_H})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA Hill coefficient above one signals apparent cooperativity but does not\nby itself determine the exact number of sites.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>Take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_d=2.0\\,\\mathrm{\\mu mol/L}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand free concentration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=4.0\\,\\mathrm{\\mu mol/L}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe occupancy is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.667<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=4/(2+4)=2/3\\approx0.667<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=K_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhalf of the independent sites are occupied. The graphic compares the\ntwo-state curve with a steeper empirical binding curve.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_020_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Langmuir occupancy and a steeper empirical Hill curve for reversible molecular binding.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Langmuir occupancy and a steeper\nempirical Hill curve for reversible molecular binding.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Theoretical curves plotted against normalised concentration; not\nbinding data.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=1\\,\\mathrm{\\mu mol/L}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_d=2\\,\\mathrm{\\mu mol/L}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngiven\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_{\\mathrm{off}}=0.2\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_{\\mathrm{on}}=10^5\\,\\mathrm{L\\,mol^{-1}s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a Hill coefficient of two not prove there are exactly two\nbinding sites?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=1/(1+2)=1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_d=0.2/10^5\\,\\mathrm{mol/L}=2\\,\\mathrm{\\mu mol/L}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The coefficient describes overall curve steepness; heterogeneity,\nallostery and experimental conditions may affect it.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The simple relation assumes independent identical sites, equilibrium\nand controlled free concentration. Multiple sites, ligand depletion,\nnonspecific adsorption and nonequilibrium kinetics can change the curve.\nThe figure is not experimental binding data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>A dissociation constant connects half occupancy to a specific\nequilibrium model. Steep curves need additional mechanistic evidence\nbefore being interpreted as site-level cooperativity.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Membranes, Osmosis and\nElectrochemical Equilibrium<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/20-011j-statistical-thermodynamics-of-biomolecular-systems-be-011j-spring-2004/\">MIT OCW – Statistical Thermodynamics of Biomolecular Systems<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: Course Description, lines 138–141; Subject relevance to lesson: M22-L2 – Molecular Binding, Chemical Potential and Cooperativity · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-592j-statistical-physics-in-biology-spring-2011/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Statistical Physics in Biology – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: chapter outline lines 34–66; Subject relevance to lesson: M22-L2 – Molecular Binding, Chemical Potential and Cooperativity · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M22-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M22-L3 – Membranes, Osmosis and Electrochemical Equilibrium<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Bachelor: Biophysics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M5: ideal gases; M6: electricity;\nM13: chemical equilibrium. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Estimate osmotic pressure,\nderive the Nernst relation and distinguish transport from\nequilibrium.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>A cell membrane separates solutions with different solute\nconcentrations. Depending on which substances can cross it, water\nexperiences osmotic pressure differences, whereas ions respond to both\nchemical and electric gradients. The membrane is not simply a mechanical\nfilter: selective permeability and transport proteins determine which\nequilibria are accessible.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>For an ideal dilute solution with effective concentration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof osmotically active solute particles, van ’t Hoff’s approximation\ngives the osmotic pressure. For a single permeant ionic species of\ncharge number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nequating electrochemical potentials,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">RT\\ln c_{\\mathrm{in}}+zF\\phi_{\\mathrm{in}}=RT\\ln c_{\\mathrm{out}}+zF\\phi_{\\mathrm{out}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngives the Nernst voltage<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Π<\/mi><mo>≃<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Pi\\simeq cRT,\\qquad E_{\\mathrm{in}}-E_{\\mathrm{out}}=\\frac{RT}{zF}\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{c_{\\mathrm{out}}}{c_{\\mathrm{in}}}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The sign convention is explicitly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi_{\\mathrm{in}}-\\phi_{\\mathrm{out}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf multiple ionic species permeate simultaneously, a coupled transport\nmodel is required and one ion’s Nernst value is not automatically the\nactual membrane voltage. Simple diffusion in a homogeneous medium\nwithout other driving forces is approximated by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=-D\\,dc/dx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>For an ideal dilute solute at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=0.10\\,\\mathrm{mol/L}=100\\,\\mathrm{mol/m^3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>298<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=298\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Π<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>8.314<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>298<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.48<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>5<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Pi\\approx100\\cdot8.314\\cdot298\\,\\mathrm{Pa}=2.48\\cdot10^5\\,\\mathrm{Pa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a monovalent positive ion with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_{\\mathrm{out}}/c_{\\mathrm{in}}=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>59.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi_{\\mathrm{in}}-\\phi_{\\mathrm{out}}\\approx+59.1\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure distinguishes an osmotic driving force from an ionic\nequilibrium voltage.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_173_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A semipermeable membrane and osmotic gradient alongside a Nernst-voltage plot versus concentration ratio.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A semipermeable membrane and osmotic\ngradient alongside a Nernst-voltage plot versus concentration\nratio.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Two distinct idealised membrane models, not measurements from a\nliving cell.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Estimate ideal osmotic pressure for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=50\\,\\mathrm{mol/m^3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=300\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the Nernst voltage for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_{\\mathrm{out}}/c_{\\mathrm{in}}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the\nK<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi><\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^+<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nNernst value insufficient for a membrane permeable to both\nK<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi><\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^+<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\nNa<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi><\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^+<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Π<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>8.314<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>125<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Pi\\approx50\\cdot8.314\\cdot300\\,\\mathrm{Pa}\\approx125\\,\\mathrm{kPa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln1=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe equilibrium voltage is zero in the single-ion model.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Both species contribute through their permeabilities and\nelectrochemical driving forces.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The osmotic relation assumes an ideal dilute solution with correctly\ncounted osmotic particles; activity and membrane-reflection effects are\nexcluded. The Nernst equation requires electrochemical equilibrium for\none ion, whereas active pumping and simultaneous fluxes require a more\ncomplete model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Osmotic pressure arises from collective solute concentration. An\nequilibrium ionic voltage instead balances chemical and electrical\npotential differences.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Polymer Chains and Entropic\nElasticity<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/20-011j-statistical-thermodynamics-of-biomolecular-systems-be-011j-spring-2004/\">MIT OCW – Statistical Thermodynamics of Biomolecular Systems<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: Course Description, lines 138–141; Subject relevance to lesson: M22-L3 – Membranes, Osmosis and Electrochemical Equilibrium · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-592j-statistical-physics-in-biology-spring-2011/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Statistical Physics in Biology – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: chapter outline lines 34–66; Subject relevance to lesson: M22-L3 – Membranes, Osmosis and Electrochemical Equilibrium · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M22-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M22-L4 – Polymer Chains and Entropic Elasticity<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Bachelor: Biophysics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M13: statistical ensembles; M22-L1:\nrandom motion. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive end-to-end distances for\nsimple polymer models and explain the statistical origin of entropic\nrestoring forces.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>A long flexible biomolecule can adopt many spatial conformations.\nMany more coiled conformations are available than fully extended\narrangements. Pulling the chain reduces the number of accessible\nconformations and hence produces an effective restoring force, even if\nthe individual idealised segments are rigid. This is entropic\nelasticity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>Take a freely jointed chain of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent segments of length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the end-to-end vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑹<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒃<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol R=\\sum_i\\boldsymbol b_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nisotropy makes the cross terms in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle R^2\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvanish, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><msup><mi>b<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle R^2\\rangle=Nb^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith many segments and small extension, the almost Gaussian distribution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑹<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∝<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>N<\/mi><msup><mi>b<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(\\boldsymbol R)\\propto\\exp[-3R^2/(2Nb^2)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the effective free energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>N<\/mi><msup><mi>b<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(R)=3k_{\\mathrm B}TR^2/(2Nb^2)+\\mathrm{const}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>f<\/mi><mo>≃<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><msup><mi>b<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>R<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{\\langle R^2\\rangle}=b\\sqrt{N},\\qquad f\\simeq\\frac{3k_{\\mathrm B}T}{Nb^2}R.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the force needed to hold the molecule at extension\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagainst the entropic pull. The linear approximation fails near the\nmaximum contour length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_c=Nb<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nReal DNA also has bending stiffness and is often modelled by a worm-like\nchain at larger forces.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsegments of length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=1\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe RMS end-to-end distance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>298<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=298\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=5\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe ideal small-extension force is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.62<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\approx0.62\\,\\mathrm{pN}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure juxtaposes schematic random-walk chains and the\nsmall-extension linear prediction.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_222_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Ideal flexible polymer conformations and an approximately linear entropic stretching force.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Ideal flexible polymer conformations and\nan approximately linear entropic stretching force.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Schematic chain shapes and theoretical curve, not a DNA pulling\nexperiment.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{\\langle R^2\\rangle}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>400<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=400<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0.5\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to the small-extension force when temperature doubles\nat fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N,b,R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the ideal Hooke-like relation fail as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napproaches\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Nb<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><msqrt><mn>400<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5\\sqrt{400}\\,\\mathrm{nm}=10\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\propto T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit doubles.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The Gaussian expression allows arbitrarily large\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbut the segment chain has a finite contour length.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The freely jointed chain neglects correlations, excluded volume,\nsolvent quality and bending rigidity. Its linear force relation requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>≪<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\ll Nb<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand sufficiently many segments. The illustrated conformations are\nschematic.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The size of an ideal random polymer scales as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSmall extension removes configurational entropy and produces a\ntemperature-dependent restoring force.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Molecular Motors, Work and\nDissipation<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-592j-statistical-physics-in-biology-spring-2011/pages/lecture-notes/statistical-physics/\">MIT OCW – Polymer Theory 1, Entropy Elasticity, Collapse<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 239–253; Subject relevance to lesson: M22-L4 – Polymer Chains and Entropic Elasticity · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-592j-statistical-physics-in-biology-spring-2011/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Statistical Physics in Biology – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: chapter outline lines 34–66; Subject relevance to lesson: M22-L4 – Polymer Chains and Entropic Elasticity · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M22-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M22-L5 – Molecular Motors, Work and Dissipation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Bachelor: Biophysics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M5: first and second laws; M13:\nnonequilibrium; M22-L4: molecular elasticity. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate mechanical work per\nstep and distinguish chemical input, efficiency and dissipated\nenergy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>Motor proteins can convert chemical free energy into directed steps\nalong filaments. Motion against an external load is not an equilibrium\nrandom walk: coupled reaction cycles, changing binding states and ATP\nturnover drive a net current. A motor can take backward as well as\nforward steps; the statistics depend on load and chemical\nconditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>A motor moving a constant opposing force\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthrough distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nperforms mechanical work\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{\\mathrm{mech}}=Fd<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a deliberately simplified cycle supplied with available chemical free\nenergy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\Delta G_{\\mathrm{chem}}|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe mechanical efficiency is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{\\mathrm{mech}}=Fd,\\qquad \\eta=\\frac{W_{\\mathrm{mech}}}{|\\Delta G_{\\mathrm{chem}}|}\\leq1.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Under isothermal conditions, useful mechanical work cannot exceed the\nsupplied free energy. The difference is dissipated via heat and internal\nreaction pathways, not necessarily observed as a local temperature rise.\nPerfect coupling would permit the energetic upper bound\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{\\max}d=|\\Delta G_{\\mathrm{chem}}|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbut a real motor’s stall load also depends on backward steps, rates and\ncoupling efficiency.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>For illustration only, suppose each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=8\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstep draws\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20k_{\\mathrm B}T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof free energy. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>298<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=298\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthis is about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8.23<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8.23\\cdot10^{-20}\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich corresponds to an ideal upper force of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10.3\\,\\mathrm{pN}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAgainst a constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=5\\,\\mathrm{pN}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nload the work is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40\\,\\mathrm{pN\\,nm}=4.0\\cdot10^{-20}\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper step, or about 49% efficiency in this model. The diagram separates\nmotor stepping from an energy-flow budget.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_179_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A model molecular motor moving stepwise along a filament with a schematic mechanical energy budget.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A model molecular motor moving stepwise\nalong a filament with a schematic mechanical energy budget.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Idealised motor energy sketch; not a measured trace of a specific\nprotein.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the work against a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{pN}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nload over one\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstep.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At eight net steps per second of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neach, find the mean velocity.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Fd=|\\Delta G|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot automatically predict a measured stall force?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>32<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=32\\,\\mathrm{pN\\,nm}=3.2\\cdot10^{-20}\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>64<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=8\\cdot8\\,\\mathrm{nm/s}=64\\,\\mathrm{nm/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It is a reversible energetic upper bound; backsteps, internal\ndissipation and chemical substeps shift the true stall load.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The model assumes constant load, a well-defined net step and one\nspecified energy input per step. Real proteins may exhibit reverse\nsteps, multiple reaction cycles, internal losses or variable step sizes.\nThe value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20k_{\\mathrm B}T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an illustrative assumption, not a universal ATP free energy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Chemical free energy can drive directed movement against a load.\nMechanical work and efficiency must be compared with a specifically\ndefined chemical energy budget.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Stochastic Reaction Networks and\nMolecular Noise<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-592j-statistical-physics-in-biology-spring-2011/pages/lecture-notes/molecular-motors/\">MIT OCW – Molecular Motors &amp; Cell Motility<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 240–247; Subject relevance to lesson: M22-L5 – Molecular Motors, Work and Dissipation · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-592j-statistical-physics-in-biology-spring-2011/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Statistical Physics in Biology – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: chapter outline lines 34–66; Subject relevance to lesson: M22-L5 – Molecular Motors, Work and Dissipation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M22-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M22-L6 – Stochastic Reaction Networks and Molecular Noise<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Bachelor: Biophysics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M13: probability and ensembles; M17:\nnumerical simulation. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model a stochastic birth–death\nprocess, compute mean molecule counts and distinguish biological noise\nfrom mean dynamics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>Some molecular species occur in only small copy numbers within an\nindividual cell. Protein production and degradation proceed through\ndiscrete random reaction events. Molecule counts therefore fluctuate in\ntime and from cell to cell even when the rates remain constant. A\ndeterministic population mean is not itself a description of every\nobserved cell.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>In a linear birth–death model, molecules are created at a constant\ntotal rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand each existing molecule disappears independently at rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe expectation satisfies<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>γ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{d\\langle n\\rangle}{dt}=k_b-\\gamma\\langle n\\rangle,\\qquad \\langle n(t)\\rangle=\\frac{k_b}{\\gamma}\\left(1-e^{-\\gamma t}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The displayed time solution assumes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle n(0)\\rangle=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor an arbitrary initial mean,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle n(t)\\rangle=n_\\infty+[\\langle n(0)\\rangle-n_\\infty]e^{-\\gamma t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_\\infty=k_b/\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe stationary distribution of this simplest model is Poisson:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msup><msubsup><mi>n<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>!<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(n)=e^{-n_\\infty}n_\\infty^n/n!<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(n)=n_\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFeedback, bursts or state-dependent rates change that result.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_b=6\\,\\mathrm{min^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=0.20\\,\\mathrm{min^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an initially empty system,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_\\infty=30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmolecules and the relaxation time is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\gamma=5\\,\\mathrm{min}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter five minutes the mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>19.0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle n\\rangle=30(1-e^{-1})\\approx19.0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe plot shows the expected mean, while the bars illustrate a\ntheoretical stationary Poisson distribution rather than single-cell\nobservations.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_022_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A time-dependent mean and a stationary Poisson count distribution for a simple birth–death reaction system.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A time-dependent mean and a stationary\nPoisson count distribution for a simple birth–death reaction\nsystem.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Synthetic theoretical curves, not single-cell\nmeasurements.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the stationary mean for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_b=4\\,\\mathrm{min^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=0.5\\,\\mathrm{min^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For these rates, find the mean after\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=2\\,\\mathrm{min}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstarting from zero.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the coefficient of variation of a stationary Poisson process\nwith mean eight?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_\\infty=k_b/\\gamma=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>5.06<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle n(2)\\rangle=8(1-e^{-1})\\approx5.06<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>8<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.354<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{CV}=\\sqrt{\\mathrm{Var}(n)}/\\langle n\\rangle=1/\\sqrt8\\approx0.354<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The model assumes constant birth rate and independent linear removal.\nIt omits regulation, cell division, extrinsic noise and multi-step\nreactions. Poisson statistics are a testable prediction of this model,\nnot a universal law of gene expression.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Discrete chemical reactions generate intrinsic noise. Mean and\nvariance measure different properties of the same system and should be\nconsidered together.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Single-Molecule Methods: FRET\nand Optical Tweezers<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-592j-statistical-physics-in-biology-spring-2011/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Statistical Physics in Biology – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: chapter outline lines 34–66; Subject relevance to lesson: M22-L6 – Stochastic Reaction Networks and Molecular Noise · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M22-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M22-L7 – Single-Molecule Methods: FRET and Optical Tweezers<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Bachelor: Biophysics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7: optics; M13: equipartition;\nM22-L4: elasticity. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Interpret idealised FRET\nefficiency in terms of distance and estimate optical trap stiffness from\nposition fluctuations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>An individual biomolecule can be studied indirectly through optical\nemission or held under load using a tightly focused optical trap. Two\ndistinct methods are Förster resonance energy transfer (FRET), which\nprobes coupling between a donor and acceptor, and optical tweezers,\nwhich use gradient forces to confine a microscopic object. Both require\ncalibration and careful treatment of noise.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>For one fixed donor–acceptor separation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a suitably defined Förster radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe ideal transfer efficiency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis distance dependent. Near its minimum, a harmonic optical trap has\npotential\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>κ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U(x)=\\kappa(x-x_0)^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEquipartition gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle U\\rangle=k_{\\mathrm B}T/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the quadratic degree of freedom; hence the measured thermal position\nvariance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndetermines its stiffness<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=\\frac{1}{1+(r/R_0)^6},\\qquad \\kappa=\\frac{k_{\\mathrm B}T}{\\sigma_x^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>FRET is not a calibration-free molecular ruler.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndepends on spectral overlap, donor quantum yield, refractive index and\nrelative dipole orientation, and apparent signals require background,\ncross-talk and detector-efficiency correction. Estimating trap stiffness\nrequires separating equilibrium thermal fluctuations from drift,\ndetector noise and finite-bandwidth effects.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>In an idealised case,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=R_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=1.2R_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.251<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E\\approx0.251<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf a trap at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>298<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=298\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a genuine thermal position standard deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_x=10\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>4.11<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0411<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa\\approx4.11\\cdot10^{-5}\\,\\mathrm{N/m}=0.0411\\,\\mathrm{pN/nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram presents theoretical FRET and harmonic-trap curves, not data\nfrom a specific biomolecule.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_265_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Nonlinear FRET efficiency versus normalised donor–acceptor distance, alongside harmonic-trap energy and thermal position spread.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Nonlinear FRET efficiency versus\nnormalised donor–acceptor distance, alongside harmonic-trap energy and\nthermal position spread.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Idealised FRET and optical-tweezer models, not experimental\nmeasurements.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find ideal FRET efficiency when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=R_0/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>κ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange if thermal position standard deviation doubles at constant\ntemperature?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does dipole orientation matter in a FRET distance estimate?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>64<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>64<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>65<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.985<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=1/(1+1/64)=64/65\\approx0.985<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa\\propto1/\\sigma_x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit falls to one quarter.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Orientation changes the Förster radius; without suitable assumptions\nor corrections, distance cannot be uniquely recovered from\nefficiency.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The FRET relation assumes a dominant donor–acceptor separation and a\ndefined\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nneglecting dynamics during integration, photophysics and detailed\ncorrections. Trap calibration assumes an equilibrium harmonic potential,\nadequate bandwidth and a calibrated detector. A licensing corrigendum\nfor the cited FRET study is not evidence of a correction to its physical\nequations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Single-molecule methods convert signals into structural or\nforce-related quantities indirectly. Their accuracy depends on an\nexplicit, verified calibration model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1140/epjp/s13360-020-00843-5\">Springer / EPJ Plus – Optical tweezers: theory and practice<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: §4.4.2, lines 593–607; Eq. 54 ff; Subject relevance to lesson: M22-L7 – Single-Molecule Methods: FRET and Optical Tweezers · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41592-018-0085-0\">Nature Methods / Hellenkamp et al. – Precision and accuracy of single-molecule FRET measurements—a multi-laboratory benchmark study<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: indexed original text, Eq. 7 and methods; PMC DOI-metadata lines 33–35; Subject relevance to lesson: M22-L7 – Single-Molecule Methods: FRET and Optical Tweezers<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-592j-statistical-physics-in-biology-spring-2011/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Statistical Physics in Biology – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: chapter outline lines 34–66; Subject relevance to lesson: M22-L7 – Single-Molecule Methods: FRET and Optical Tweezers · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
